**Step 1 — Add `JpaSpecificationExecutor<T>` to the repository:**

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long>,
        JpaSpecificationExecutor<Order> { }
```

**Step 2 — Define each filter as a static `Specification<T>` factory:**

```java
public class OrderSpecs {
    public static Specification<Order> hasStatus(String status) {
        return (root, q, cb) -> cb.equal(root.get("status"), status);
    }
    public static Specification<Order> createdAfter(LocalDate date) {
        return (root, q, cb) -> cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("createdAt"), date);
    }
}
```

**Step 3 — Compose at the service layer with `Specification.where(...)`:**

```java
Specification<Order> spec = Specification
    .where(filter.status() != null ? OrderSpecs.hasStatus(filter.status()) : null)
    .and(filter.from()   != null ? OrderSpecs.createdAfter(filter.from())  : null);
repo.findAll(spec, pageable);
```

**Why this beats derived methods:**
- **One method handles every filter combination** — no combinatorial explosion.
- **`null` predicates are ignored** by `Specification.where(...)` chains.
- **Reusable** — the same `hasStatus` Specification powers `findAll`, `count`, and `findOne`.
- **Testable** — predicate factories are unit-testable in isolation.
- **Parameterized SQL** — Criteria API values become bind parameters; no injection risk.

Order search with status, city, date range, customer name (join), with pagination and a fetch join.

```java
public class OrderSpecs {
    public static Specification<Order> hasStatus(OrderStatus status) {
        return (root, q, cb) -> status == null ? null : cb.equal(root.get("status"), status);
    }

    public static Specification<Order> inCity(String city) {
        return (root, q, cb) -> city == null || city.isBlank() ? null
            : cb.equal(root.get("shippingCity"), city);
    }

    public static Specification<Order> createdBetween(Instant from, Instant to) {
        return (root, q, cb) -> {
            if (from == null && to == null) return null;
            if (from == null) return cb.lessThanOrEqualTo(root.get("createdAt"), to);
            if (to == null)   return cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("createdAt"), from);
            return cb.between(root.get("createdAt"), from, to);
        };
    }

    public static Specification<Order> customerNameContains(String fragment) {
        return (root, q, cb) -> {
            if (fragment == null || fragment.isBlank()) return null;
            Join<Order, Customer> customer = root.join("customer", JoinType.INNER);
            return cb.like(cb.lower(customer.get("name")),
                           "%" + fragment.toLowerCase() + "%");
        };
    }

    public static Specification<Order> fetchCustomerAndItems() {
        return (root, q, cb) -> {
            if (Long.class != q.getResultType()) {
                root.fetch("customer", JoinType.LEFT);
                root.fetch("items", JoinType.LEFT);
                q.distinct(true);
            }
            return null;
        };
    }
}

@Service
public class OrderSearchService {
    public Page<Order> search(OrderFilter f, Pageable page) {
        Specification<Order> spec = Specification
            .where(OrderSpecs.hasStatus(f.status()))
            .and(OrderSpecs.inCity(f.city()))
            .and(OrderSpecs.createdBetween(f.from(), f.to()))
            .and(OrderSpecs.customerNameContains(f.customerName()))
            .and(OrderSpecs.fetchCustomerAndItems());
        return orderRepo.findAll(spec, page);
    }
}
```

**Two subtleties:**
- **`Long.class != q.getResultType()`** guards the fetch so it runs only on the data query — fetches on the count query are forbidden.
- **`q.distinct(true)`** deduplicates parents when fetching `@OneToMany` — otherwise one order with 10 items returns 10 rows.

Spring Data has no built-in keyset pagination as of 3.x; it's straightforward with `@Query`.

```java
public interface FeedRepository extends JpaRepository<Post, Long> {

    @Query("""
        SELECT p FROM Post p
        WHERE (:cursorCreatedAt IS NULL OR
               p.createdAt < :cursorCreatedAt OR
               (p.createdAt = :cursorCreatedAt AND p.id < :cursorId))
        ORDER BY p.createdAt DESC, p.id DESC
    """)
    List<Post> findNextPage(@Param("cursorCreatedAt") Instant cursorCreatedAt,
                            @Param("cursorId") Long cursorId,
                            Pageable limit);
}

@RestController
public class FeedController {
    @GetMapping("/feed")
    public FeedResponse feed(@RequestParam(required = false) String cursor,
                             @RequestParam(defaultValue = "20") int size) {
        Cursor c = cursor == null ? Cursor.empty() : Cursor.decode(cursor);
        List<Post> posts = feedRepo.findNextPage(c.createdAt(), c.id(),
            PageRequest.of(0, size));
        String nextCursor = posts.isEmpty() ? null
            : Cursor.of(posts.get(posts.size() - 1)).encode();
        return new FeedResponse(posts, nextCursor);
    }
}
```

- **Cursor is base64-encoded `(createdAt, id)`** — sent to client, echoed on next request.
- **Client never sees page numbers** — only 'next' cursors.
- **Performance stays flat** even at post 10,000,000.
